WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:04.400
Static Shock returns to television?&nbsp;
A secret crossover with Barbie?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:06.960
What mysteries are hiding in&nbsp;
the dreams of "The Sandman?"

00:00:08.160 --> 00:00:12.640
When we first meet Johanna Constantine, she's&nbsp;
on her way to a church for some light exorcism&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.640 --> 00:00:16.160
under the pretext of officiating a&nbsp;
clandestine wedding. When she arrives,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.160 --> 00:00:19.200
instead of marrying the couple,&nbsp;
she speaks a Latin incantation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.200 --> 00:00:23.200
that forces a demon from the possessed&nbsp;
groom. Things get messy from there.

00:00:23.200 --> 00:00:28.000
Once the demon erupts from the man he possessed,&nbsp;
Dream arrives and identifies him as Agilieth.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:31.440
Before Dream can get the information&nbsp;
he needs, Johanna completes her spell,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.440 --> 00:00:34.960
and forces the demon back to Hell,&nbsp;
much to our main character's annoyance.

00:00:35.520 --> 00:00:40.080
Agilieth doesn't appear in "The Sandman" comics,&nbsp;
but fans of John Constantine may recall him from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.080 --> 00:00:46.240
"Hellblazer " #196. In that issue, John is deeply&nbsp;
unfocused after recovering from being hypnotized,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.240 --> 00:00:50.480
and he's unable to recover his memories. He&nbsp;
stumbles upon a horrific scene where demons&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.480 --> 00:00:55.040
sit around a table eating people. At the head&nbsp;
of the table is Rosacarnis, who welcomes him,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.040 --> 00:01:01.120
but Agilieth has other ideas. He stands, defying&nbsp;
John despite his host's insistence to sit down.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.760 --> 00:01:06.080
Rosacarnis rips the demon apart from the&nbsp;
inside, destroying him at John's feet.

00:01:07.120 --> 00:01:10.960
One of the recurring themes of "The Sandman"&nbsp;
is that everyone gets a single life to live,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.960 --> 00:01:14.400
and then they die and move on.&nbsp;
For some, it's Hell, for others,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.400 --> 00:01:18.880
it's the Silver City. Those are two examples&nbsp;
of many. As far as the Dreaming is concerned,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.880 --> 00:01:22.800
when a person dies there, they can become&nbsp;
a raven, as demonstrated by Matthew.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.800 --> 00:01:27.920
But there are exceptions. Hob Gadling lucked&nbsp;
into immortality by becoming the focus of a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.920 --> 00:01:31.840
bet between Dream and Death, but he's not&nbsp;
the only immortal human in the series.

00:01:31.840 --> 00:01:36.160
Another immortal soul is briefly seen in&nbsp;
Episode 3, and that would be Mad Hettie.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.160 --> 00:01:40.240
She meets Johanna Constantine and&nbsp;
tells her that Morpheus has returned.

00:01:40.240 --> 00:01:45.120
"He's no fairy story, missy. He's&nbsp;
back, and he wants his sand."

00:01:45.120 --> 00:01:49.760
Most viewers probably wondered who this woman is&nbsp;
and how she could know anything about Morpheus'&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.760 --> 00:01:54.960
activities. Mad Hettie is actually more&nbsp;
than two hundred years old, born in 1741,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.960 --> 00:01:59.760
and like Mr. Gadling, she didn't think dying&nbsp;
was all that interesting a prospect. To avoid&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.760 --> 00:02:04.480
her eventual demise, she hid her heart from Death,&nbsp;
literally. She's been labeled a "witch" over the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.480 --> 00:02:09.360
years and does know some basic magic. She pops up&nbsp;
occasionally to interact with John Constantine,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.360 --> 00:02:13.520
just as she does with his female counterpart&nbsp;
in "The Sandman" series. Other than offering&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.520 --> 00:02:17.520
up her incredible age, she tends to keep&nbsp;
her most important secrets to herself.

00:02:18.320 --> 00:02:21.280
When the Corinthian leaves Jed&nbsp;
in the hotel room in Episode 9,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.280 --> 00:02:25.360
Jed is watching cartoons on television.&nbsp;
On the screen a few characters flash by,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.360 --> 00:02:29.280
revealing that he's enjoying an episode of&nbsp;
"Static Shock" called "Toys in the Hood."

00:02:29.280 --> 00:02:34.080
In the episode, the Toyman arrives in Static's&nbsp;
hometown of Dakota and attempts to capture Darci.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.080 --> 00:02:38.320
This is important because Darci is an&nbsp;
android created by the Toyman. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.320 --> 00:02:42.720
she seems to have developed a mind of her own and&nbsp;
distances herself from the creepy ne'er-do-well.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.280 --> 00:02:47.840
After Toyman successfully abducts Darci, Static&nbsp;
goes searching for her. He's soon joined by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.840 --> 00:02:52.000
Superman and the two team up for the episode.&nbsp;
It doesn't take long for the heroes to learn&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.000 --> 00:02:57.200
that the Toyman's plot isn't what it appears. His&nbsp;
true motive is to capture Static's friend Daisy.

00:02:57.200 --> 00:03:02.320
The Toyman and Darci plan to use Daisy's body as&nbsp;
a template so that the android can become human.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.320 --> 00:03:06.480
Several elements of the cartoon align with&nbsp;
Jed's story in "The Sandman." Like Daisy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.480 --> 00:03:11.680
Jed is caught between two powerful forces. On the&nbsp;
one side, the manipulative Corinthian hopes to use&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.680 --> 00:03:16.640
Jed for his nefarious purposes, and on the other,&nbsp;
stands Jed's sister Rose and her friend Gilbert.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.640 --> 00:03:20.720
The two are working together to try and&nbsp;
save Jed. Of course, there's more going on,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.720 --> 00:03:24.400
but the crux of the "Static Shock" episode&nbsp;
parallels the events of the episode.

00:03:25.280 --> 00:03:29.680
As Dream and Matthew are escorted through Hell,&nbsp;
they happen upon a woman locked inside a cage.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.680 --> 00:03:33.520
When Dream sees her, the two have a short&nbsp;
conversation, and it's clear from their talk&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.520 --> 00:03:37.920
that they have a long history. Dream, who Nada&nbsp;
knows as "Kai'ckul," reveals that they haven't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.920 --> 00:03:42.720
seen each other in 10,000 years. She begs for&nbsp;
Dream to free her, but he's not yet ready.

00:03:43.280 --> 00:03:49.486
"I still love you, but I&nbsp;
have not yet forgiven you."

00:03:50.000 --> 00:03:54.240
His response is chilling and taken directly&nbsp;
from the comics. The scene will leave viewers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.240 --> 00:03:59.120
wondering how Morpheus could be so callous to&nbsp;
a woman he loves, but there is an explanation.

00:03:59.120 --> 00:04:04.160
Nada's damnation came about following a brief&nbsp;
affair between her and the Dream Lord. She tracked&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.160 --> 00:04:08.560
Dream to the Dreaming and confessed her love for&nbsp;
him, but fled after discovering that he was an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.560 --> 00:04:13.600
Endless. Filled with passion, Dream followed her&nbsp;
to the Waking World and rekindled her affection.

00:04:13.600 --> 00:04:18.240
While making love on a hill outside her city, a&nbsp;
meteorite slams into her home, destroying everyone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.240 --> 00:04:23.600
and everything. The devastation consumes Nada and&nbsp;
she commits suicide before Dream can stop her.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.600 --> 00:04:28.400
Morpheus follows her into the afterlife and asks&nbsp;
her to return with him. After refusing twice,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.400 --> 00:04:32.160
he tells her if she does it again,&nbsp;
he'll damn her to Hell, which he does.

00:04:32.160 --> 00:04:36.320
Eventually, Dream comes to terms with Nada's&nbsp;
actions after an ordeal with a demon and places&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.320 --> 00:04:40.880
her soul in a newborn baby. Then he tells Nada she&nbsp;
can return to the Dreaming whenever she wishes.

00:04:41.680 --> 00:04:46.400
When Rose's abilities as a Dream Vortex begin to&nbsp;
manifest, she finds herself inside the dreams of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.400 --> 00:04:50.000
her friends who live with her in a quaint&nbsp;
house in Florida. Each of the residents'&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.000 --> 00:04:54.240
dreams is important to the story, but one&nbsp;
stands out from the rest. Her friend Barbie's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.240 --> 00:04:58.480
dream seems to have a long history filled with&nbsp;
familiar characters and a developing story.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.480 --> 00:05:02.800
In the dream, Barbie is on a quest with a&nbsp;
traveling companion called Martin Tenbones,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.800 --> 00:05:07.440
who mentions something called the "Porpentine."&nbsp;
Assuming the show stays true to the comics,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.440 --> 00:05:11.680
this should become important to the story&nbsp;
following the events of "The Sandman" Season 1.

00:05:11.680 --> 00:05:16.080
Barbie's dream, Martin Tenbones, and the&nbsp;
Porpentine all set up the comics arc titled&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.080 --> 00:05:20.640
"A Game of You." The fairytale-like environs&nbsp;
of Barbie's subconscious are layered with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.640 --> 00:05:25.440
complexity and go back to her childhood. She&nbsp;
dreams of a magical world called The Land,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.440 --> 00:05:30.240
where she's a deposed princess fighting against&nbsp;
the evil abstract being known as the Cuckoo.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.240 --> 00:05:34.720
As Barbie matures, her dream continues, but&nbsp;
everything falls apart after she witnesses her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.720 --> 00:05:38.720
husband Ken having sex with a dream woman.&nbsp;
Barbie eventually moves to New York City,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.720 --> 00:05:41.840
where she no longer dreams,&nbsp;
leaving The Land in disarray.

00:05:41.840 --> 00:05:45.440
As The Land crumbles, Martin Tenbones&nbsp;
breaks into the Waking World looking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.440 --> 00:05:49.120
for Barbie's help. Perhaps we'll see this&nbsp;
storyline in future seasons of the show.

00:05:50.320 --> 00:05:54.240
Jenna Coleman's casting as Johanna Constantine&nbsp;
is a change from the source material,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.240 --> 00:05:58.720
as she replaces John Constantine from the&nbsp;
comics. They're more or less the same character,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.720 --> 00:06:01.840
and Coleman plays a perfectly&nbsp;
damaged supernatural sleuth.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.840 --> 00:06:06.560
If you enjoyed seeing her as Johanna Constantine&nbsp;
in the third episode, it's something of a treat&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.560 --> 00:06:11.360
to see her return in the sixth as the Lady&nbsp;
Johanna Constantine, her 18th-century ancestor.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.360 --> 00:06:14.800
She and her band of ruffians threaten&nbsp;
Morpheus and are quickly dispatched.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.800 --> 00:06:19.840
Later, Morpheus mentions seeing her once more,&nbsp;
explaining that she completed a task for him.

00:06:19.840 --> 00:06:24.640
Constantine will likely return at some point down&nbsp;
the line because Lady Johanna's task is essential&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.640 --> 00:06:30.400
to the overall story. In 1794, Dream calls in&nbsp;
a favor Constantine owes him and asks that she&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.400 --> 00:06:36.080
rescue his son, Orpheus. Morpheus' son was ripped&nbsp;
apart at one point, but being immortal, continued&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.080 --> 00:06:41.440
living as only a head. He was ultimately buried&nbsp;
in a pile of corpses from the French Revolution.

00:06:41.440 --> 00:06:45.920
Johanna finds Orpheus' head and returns&nbsp;
it to Morpheus, completing her task.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.920 --> 00:06:50.400
After their deal is done, Lady Constantine goes on&nbsp;
with the rest of her life, keeping her family name&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.400 --> 00:06:55.600
going so that one day, John Constantine could&nbsp;
be born. Lady Johanna died at the age of 99,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.600 --> 00:06:59.720
and Dream had her body interred near&nbsp;
Orpheus' temple on the island of Naxos.

00:07:00.720 --> 00:07:05.520
In the fifth episode, "24/7," the character Judy&nbsp;
is introduced as a woman concerned over the state&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.520 --> 00:07:10.240
of her relationship with her partner Donna. Judy&nbsp;
admits to hitting Donna during a tumultuous fight,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.240 --> 00:07:14.240
and all indications suggest their time&nbsp;
together is at an end. The episode becomes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.240 --> 00:07:18.400
a nightmare wrought by Dream's ruby, and&nbsp;
Donna is mentioned throughout the episode.

00:07:18.400 --> 00:07:22.800
In Episode 7, the Corinthian picks up a&nbsp;
photograph of Judy in Rose Walker's apartment,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.800 --> 00:07:27.120
showing that the two know each other. That's the&nbsp;
only reference tying the two together, but far&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.120 --> 00:07:32.000
more is revealed in the comics. Donna's full name&nbsp;
is Donna Cavanagh, and she doesn't make her first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.000 --> 00:07:36.560
appearance until the issue, "Slaughter on Fifth&nbsp;
Avenue" during the "A Game of You" storyline.

00:07:36.560 --> 00:07:41.120
Donna's backstory is expanded to explain her&nbsp;
relationship with Judy. After Judy's death,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.120 --> 00:07:45.760
she started going by the name "Foxglove,"&nbsp;
which she used in her singer-songwriter career.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:45.760 --> 00:07:48.240
She's one of the people living&nbsp;
with Barbie in New York City,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.240 --> 00:07:51.360
and she ventures into her dream to&nbsp;
help rescue Barbie from the Cuckoo.

00:07:52.400 --> 00:07:56.000
Dream ventures into Hell with as&nbsp;
much respect for Lucifer as possible,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.000 --> 00:08:00.480
but things don't work out quite as intended.&nbsp;
He manages to embarrass Lucifer in Hell,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.480 --> 00:08:05.360
in front of every demon which doesn't sit well&nbsp;
with the Morningstar. After Dream leaves, Lucifer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:05.360 --> 00:08:11.840
promises to do something unprecedented that will&nbsp;
make God angry, and complete her ultimate goal.

00:08:11.840 --> 00:08:14.080
"...and bring Morpheus to his knees."

00:08:14.080 --> 00:08:17.760
If things play out as they did in the&nbsp;
comics, Lucifer's plan is well known.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.760 --> 00:08:21.360
In the books, Dream returns to Hell to&nbsp;
forgive Nada, but he finds the realm&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.360 --> 00:08:26.560
empty. Only Lucifer remains, searching for any&nbsp;
lost souls or demons remaining in damnation.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.560 --> 00:08:31.200
Lucifer explains that it was time to quit being&nbsp;
the Lord of Hell, and he's ready for retirement.

00:08:31.200 --> 00:08:34.800
Dream helps to cut off Lucifer's wings&nbsp;
and is left holding the key to Hell.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.800 --> 00:08:38.800
But this was Lucifer's plan: to saddle&nbsp;
Dream with the burden of damnation.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:38.800 --> 00:08:43.520
Lucifer explains that it was Dream who provided&nbsp;
the impetus to abdicate. Handing Dream the key,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:43.520 --> 00:08:47.680
Lucifer reminds him of the erstwhile vow&nbsp;
to destroy the Dream Lord, explaining,

00:08:47.680 --> 00:08:48.960
"Perhaps it will destroy you,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.960 --> 00:08:52.080
and perhaps it won't. But I doubt&nbsp;
it will make your life any easier."

00:08:53.356 --> 00:08:56.800
The "Sandman" comics go into detail&nbsp;
regarding the nature of the Endless,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:56.800 --> 00:08:59.600
who they are, and what they&nbsp;
do. The Netflix series only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:59.600 --> 00:09:02.960
offers a glimpse of the Endless with&nbsp;
depictions of Dream, Death, Desire,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.960 --> 00:09:07.840
and Despair. The Endless are the personification&nbsp;
of the concepts the siblings are named after.

00:09:07.840 --> 00:09:11.840
The Endless are the masters of their own&nbsp;
domains, and Dream's is called the Dreaming.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:11.840 --> 00:09:16.000
There are others each member of the family&nbsp;
rules over, though Dream's is the one most often&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.000 --> 00:09:20.400
visited by sentient beings. Most everyone&nbsp;
meets Death, but that happens only once,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.400 --> 00:09:25.280
while those who sleep spend a third of their lives&nbsp;
in Dream's realm, making him incredibly powerful.

00:09:25.280 --> 00:09:29.120
Each member of the Endless has a gallery&nbsp;
within their realm. It's from the gallery&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:29.120 --> 00:09:33.600
that they contact one another by holding their&nbsp;
siblings' sigil and asking for an audience.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:33.600 --> 00:09:38.560
Only Destiny can compel his siblings to a&nbsp;
meeting, whereas the rest must make a request.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:38.560 --> 00:09:41.440
The family consists of seven&nbsp;
members. In addition to Dream,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:41.440 --> 00:09:45.280
Death, Desire, Despair, and Destiny,&nbsp;
there's Delirium and Destruction.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:45.280 --> 00:09:49.840
If you pay attention to the galleries in the&nbsp;
show, you'll notice one portrait remains empty.

00:09:50.560 --> 00:09:54.560
At several points throughout the first season&nbsp;
of "The Sandman," various members of the Endless&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:54.560 --> 00:09:58.640
mention someone they call "the Prodigal."&nbsp;
It's never explained who they're referring to,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:58.640 --> 00:10:02.000
but it's something you can figure out&nbsp;
whether you've read the comics or not.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:02.000 --> 00:10:05.920
The name itself is a clue, as "prodigal"&nbsp;
means a son or daughter who abandons his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:05.920 --> 00:10:09.680
familial obligations to set out on&nbsp;
their own. Knowing this and seeing an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:09.680 --> 00:10:14.320
empty portrait in Dream and Desire's galleries,&nbsp;
it's clear the Prodigal is one of the Endless.

00:10:14.320 --> 00:10:18.800
The comics reveal that the Prodigal is&nbsp;
Destruction, who goes by the name Olethros.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:18.800 --> 00:10:23.920
He is the only member of the family to abandon&nbsp;
his post, leaving his responsibilities behind.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:23.920 --> 00:10:27.040
This is why the Endless refer to&nbsp;
him as "Prodigal," and it's also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:27.040 --> 00:10:30.401
why his portrait doesn't hold&nbsp;
a sigil in anyone's galleries.

00:10:30.578 --> 00:10:32.751
"The Prodigal has returned?"

00:10:32.751 --> 00:10:38.800
"What? Oh. Him? No, no, he's still missing."

00:10:39.440 --> 00:10:42.960
Destruction's purpose is to destroy&nbsp;
so new things can be created,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:42.960 --> 00:10:47.040
but he abandoned his responsibilities when&nbsp;
mankind turned towards science. Knowing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:47.040 --> 00:10:51.200
humanity would make new and horrific means of&nbsp;
destruction, Destruction opted to walk away,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:51.200 --> 00:10:55.600
which he did some time in the 17th century.&nbsp;
He fell off his siblings' radars and traveled&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:55.600 --> 00:11:00.320
with a talking dog named Barnabas. He ultimately&nbsp;
turns up again, but his convictions never change.

00:11:01.440 --> 00:11:06.000
At the end of Season 1, Rose leaves the Dreaming,&nbsp;
she awakens to learn that her friend Lyta has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:06.000 --> 00:11:10.800
given birth to a son. She meets the baby, but the&nbsp;
child doesn't have a name. Of course, the comics&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:10.800 --> 00:11:15.040
spell it all out, and the child is incredibly&nbsp;
important to the story and Dream's fate.

00:11:15.040 --> 00:11:18.800
The boy is named Daniel Hall, and as&nbsp;
you probably remember from the show,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:18.800 --> 00:11:22.560
Dream insists the baby is his. This&nbsp;
doesn't sit well with Rose or Lyta,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:22.560 --> 00:11:26.240
but it's not expanded upon, leaving&nbsp;
many wondering what he's talking about.

00:11:26.240 --> 00:11:30.000
Daniel was created inside the Dreaming,&nbsp;
making him part of Dream's domain.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:30.000 --> 00:11:33.600
He's the son of Hector Hall, who in&nbsp;
the comics is known as Doctor Fate,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:33.600 --> 00:11:38.240
and Lyta, but he's destined for something more.&nbsp;
Dream is the one who names Lyta's son Daniel,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:38.240 --> 00:11:42.080
which she likes despite Dream's claim on&nbsp;
the child. It doesn't take long for Daniel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:42.080 --> 00:11:46.880
to manifest an ability to enter the Dreaming&nbsp;
in a manner far more lucid than most dreamers.

00:11:46.880 --> 00:11:51.520
Ultimately, Lyta loses her sanity and seeks&nbsp;
out the Furies to exact their form of justice&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:51.520 --> 00:11:55.440
on Dream. The Furies destroy Dream for&nbsp;
killing Orpheus, though he granted his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:55.440 --> 00:12:00.320
son a peaceful end. This in turn leaves&nbsp;
the role of Dream Lord open. Soon after,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:00.320 --> 00:12:04.495
Daniel artificially ages, and becomes&nbsp;
the new manifestation of Dream.

